capability record/parse-text  a record read from a zone file's text
  heroes  pending
  go      -
  rust    hickory_proto::serialize::txt::Parser
  python  dns.rdata.from_text, dns.rrset.from_text, dns.zone.from_text
  note    The master file format (RFC 1035 § 5), its `$ORIGIN`, `$TTL`, parentheses and relative names, is not read

capability message/text  a whole message written as dig or dnspython writes it
  heroes  pending
  go      -
  rust    Display for Message
  python  Message.to_text
  note    No tool on this machine writes a whole message's text to be observed: dnspython is absent, and dig's sections carry comments and timings of its own

capability client/resolver  a resolver: its servers, search list, deadline, tries and options
  heroes  dns.Resolver, dns.resolver
  go      net.Resolver, net.Resolver.PreferGo, net.Resolver.StrictErrors, net.DefaultResolver
  rust    hickory_resolver::Resolver, ResolverConfig, ResolverOpts, NameServerConfig
  python  dns.resolver.Resolver, Resolver.nameservers, Resolver.search, Resolver.ndots, Resolver.timeout, Resolver.lifetime, Resolver.rotate, Resolver.use_edns
  note    `StrictErrors` is the one behaviour: a failure that is not `not_found` ends a search at once

capability client/system-config  the system's resolvers read: /etc/resolv.conf
  heroes  dns.system, dns.parse_resolv_conf
  go      net's reading of /etc/resolv.conf (internal)
  rust    hickory_resolver::system_conf::read_system_conf, system_conf::parse_resolv_conf
  python  dns.resolver.Resolver(configure=True), Resolver.read_resolv_conf
  note    glibc's and Go's defaults: two tries, five seconds each, `ndots:1`, and the loopback servers where the file names none

capability client/system-config-windows  the system's resolvers on Windows, which no file holds
  heroes  pending
  go      net's reading of GetAdaptersAddresses (internal)
  rust    system_conf on Windows (ipconfig)
  python  dns.resolver.Resolver on Windows (the registry, WMI)
  note    Windows keeps its servers in the registry and in its adapters' settings, and no file holds them; reading them needs a member of the floor (GetAdaptersAddresses in `hero_net.h`), which this package may not write. `dns.system` fails `unsupported` there and a program names its servers with `dns.resolver`

capability client/exchange  a message sent to a server and its answer read: UDP, TCP where it was truncated, tries, a deadline each
  heroes  dns.exchange
  go      net.Resolver.Dial
  rust    hickory_client::Client, DnsHandle::send, UdpClientStream, TcpClientStream
  python  dns.query.udp, dns.query.tcp, dns.query.udp_with_fallback, dns.query.send_udp, dns.query.receive_udp, dns.query.send_tcp, dns.query.receive_tcp
  note    The ID is fresh from the system's generator on every send and the port is the system's choice, RFC 5452; a datagram whose sender, ID or question differs is ignored, never taken

capability client/stream  a message exchanged over a stream the program opened: DNS over TLS through `tls`
  heroes  dns.exchange_stream
  go      -
  rust    hickory's `TlsClientStream`
  python  dns.query.tls

capability client/doh  DNS over HTTPS
  heroes  refused: `http` stands a layer above `dns` (README § A1), so this package cannot send one; a program does, in three calls: `dns.encode` of a `dns.new_query` (its ID 0) as the body of an `http` POST of type `application/dns-message`, and `dns.decode` of the answer's body (RFC 8484)
  go      -
  rust    hickory's `HttpsClientStream`
  python  dns.query.https

